<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Bey al-Kabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1773">1773</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali Bey al-Kabir was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> bey of Georgian origin who became the dominant power in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the 1760s. In 1768 he deposed the Ottoman governor and acted as an independent ruler, crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and sending armies into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bey-abu-al-dhahab">Muhammad Bey Abu al-Dhahab</a> turned against him, and he died of wounds in 1773.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir.jpg" alt="Ali Bey al-Kabir" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Bey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
